export const body = "<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>A Rough Start</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Technical challenges</li><li>One of the harshest clients ever</li><li>The outbreak shutdown</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>How we turned the tide</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Personalized training</li><li>Mutual learning</li><li>Multilayered quality check</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The calm after the storm</li><li>Lessons learnt</li><li>Closing words</li></ul>\n\n<p>So far, our best practices have come exclusively and the studio. This time, our story originates in the studio. Unlike our previous stories, this project had (for the lack of a better word) an awful start. Due to a few missteps in the initial delivery, the team was met with vitriolic reactions from the client's side. So, in this article, we will walk you through the story from the beginning till the end, when they pull a spectacular recovery.<br></p>\n\n<h3>A Rough Start</h3>\n\n<h4>Technical challenges</h4>\n\n<p>Regarding the scope of work, the studio was responsible for building and props creation, along with tree asset optimization. There were two major technical challenges for them: SpeedTree and Xbox.</p>\n\n<p>While SpeedTree wasn't an unfamiliar tool to the team, they had only used it for cinematics creation. For this project, they needed to use this software to create and optimize tree assets for in-game purposes. Besides, this title was an Xbox exclusive, and the team also didn't have much experience with the platform. So, their TAs had to reach out to other studios to study SpeedTree and Xbox's system usage for optimization.</p>\n\n<p>Together, the whole team discussed the pipeline and updated it dynamically. As for documentation, the TAs created XBOX measurement-related docs while artists created SpeedTree optimization methods and standards. In short, the TAs have made a great contribution to this project.<br></p>\n\n<h4>One of the harshest clients ever</h4>\n\n<p>For quality check, the client assigned a Technical Director to communicate with the team, and this person was extremely strict. Not only was he demanding in terms of quality, but he also didn't hesitate to make ruthless comments. The team noted that it was important for them to be calm and respond to him with respect. They didn't look at the harsh feedback through a pessimistic lens. Instead, they viewed it as a reminder to win back the client's trust.</p>\n\n<p>We would like to quote a couple of the TD's comments to give you a general feel of the situation:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Quote number 1</strong></p></td><td><p>\"I'm really not happy with what you delivered. There’s serious and obvious mistakes made, which give me the impression that no one on your side took a look ,at what was delivered...</p>\n<p>...I stopped there and even didn’t wanted to check the Unreal files, as I spent already several hours to check and review absolutely not acceptable work.\"</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Quote number 2</strong></p></td><td><p>\"I’m not happy mainly because from now you lost completely my trust, it means that for every delivery I’ll have to spent much more time than I should to verify absolutely everything to be sure that all data are in a correct state, and I’m already over busy.\"</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h4>The outbreak shutdown</h4>\n\n<p>Earlier this year when the virus started to spread across China, all of the production teams in China had to take immediate measures. So, everyone in the studio was asked to work from home. This sudden, unfortunate downtime not only slowed down the production but also hurt artists' turnovers. Moreover, newly-joined artists at this time didn't have proper preparation to understand the benchmark.</p>\n\n<p>Communication was of utmost importance during this stage. The team updated their situation regarding the pandemic as frequently as possible. They tried to be transparent and sincere, always letting the client know that they could do better if given the opportunity.</p>\n\n<h3>How we turned the tide</h3>\n\n<p>The best thing about starting at the bottom is everything can only get better. Understanding their position, the team knew they couldn't afford any other mistake; they had to perfect every delivery afterwards. First, they used the pilot to set the quality bar.</p>\n\n<p>Second, they took three measures to ensure quality consistency: <strong>personalized training</strong>,<strong> mutual learning</strong>, and <strong>multilayered quality check</strong>.</p>\n\n<h4>Personalized training</h4>\n\n<p>During the work-from-home period, the higher-ups dedicated their time to employ separate training for each artist. The training program consisted of documents, quality references, case studies, feedback collection, and more. Each of these items was customized according to the artists' individual skills and understanding of the project.</p>\n\n<p>Our team also had the mentor program similar to that of the <strong>project</strong>'s team. In this program, one senior is paired with one junior; the senior will be responsible for both of them. Working in pairs like this enhances the team spirit, develop a cross-checking habit, and allow people to learn from one another. The team believes that even junior artists, despite their lack of experience, still have valuable knowledge to share. Besides, young people tend to be more experimental and energetic, which could bring some positive energy to the discussions.</p>\n\n<p>However, unlike the project's approach, the mentors in this project didn't need to be seniors; as long as that artist can deliver consistently, he can be considered for the role. And that leads us to mutual learning.</p>\n\n<h4>Mutual learning</h4>\n\n<p>Perhaps, the most successful outcome of their initial failure was their newfound sharing habit. By blurring the line between people of different skill levels, the team created an inclusive environment, in which all team members are free to voice their opinions.</p>\n\n<p>Furthermore, there were daily stand-up meetings over the course of the production. At the beginning of each workday, the team would come together and give updates on their part: what was done yesterday, what to be done today, what are the issues to address, how to address them, etc. These routine gatherings unified the team members, turning them into a cohesive unit.</p>\n\n<p>Other than this daily routine, the team also held informal sharing sessions, in which they went to a person's desk and talked about a small topic like friends. They had no fixed schedule for this activity; these sessions just happened when someone had something to share. So to maintain this activity, the team tried to:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><ul><li>Keep the topics of discussion small<em> </em>and project-related (something to be solved and improved upon)</li><li>Allow everyone to contribute during the discussions</li><li>Host the sessions casually (in this case, at someone's desk)</li><li>Let TLs observe and pick the potential candidate to host the next session</li><li>Make people feel proud of their sharing</li></ul></div>\n\n<p>Last but not least, there was daily life sharing. If an artist encounters a familiar sight—something that could be in their project, they will take a photo and send it to their WeChat's group. Their sharing activities went beyond the regular working hours because they truly enjoyed learning new things and learning from their teammates. The sharing was boundless.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stand-up meeting</strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d648f72f5d6439a09b0f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d648f72f5d6439a09b0f.webp\" width=\"1049\" height=\"788\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>WeChat screenshots</strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5372c062593068bf2cea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5372c062593068bf2cea.webp\" width=\"663\" height=\"718\" style=\"--source-width:424px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Multilayered quality check</h4>\n\n<p>The team established an airtight QC process consisting of multiple phases and involving everyone in production. This entire process was recorded in Hansoft and made transparent to all team members. Thus, everyone—from artists to leads, AD, TAs, and AP—was on the same page and willing to devote their time to this QC process. Despite having multiple layers, the team said it didn't take a lot of time to carry out, especially once they got used to it.</p>\n\n<p>team also decided to do the delivery check midday. That means people would have time to digest the feedback and rework their assets accordingly within the same day—when their memory is still fresh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Multilayered Quality Check System</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4ab43f0129569e2c34c5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4ab43f0129569e2c34c5.webp\" width=\"560\" height=\"468\" style=\"--source-width:560px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>The Hansoft's records</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0452f928c4c289874280.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0452f928c4c289874280.webp\" width=\"751\" height=\"847\" style=\"--source-width:481px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>The calm after the storm</h3>\n\n<p>After a long period of ceaselessly improving their quality, the negative feedback decreased, the satisfaction increased, and trust was regained. The team went from creating only 1 building, 1 vehicle, and optimizing tree assets to create a diverse range of trees and buildings.</p>\n\n<p>The diversity here doesn't simply refer to the stylistic choice of the title; it also refers to the different settings and cultures these assets reside in. There were buildings from Japan, English, and other European countries. As for the trees, the artists had to capture their visual characteristics during the Spring season (when the game takes place). Fortunately, the studio is a culturally diverse place: one of the ADs had lived in Japan for a while, so he had a strong understanding about Japanese buildings; they had one AD (not working on the project) who comes from England to tell them about the Big Ben tower; so on and so forth<strong>.</strong></p>\n\n<p>The client eventually cancelled other vendors to work exclusively with the studio since this September, the client has changed the contract type from asset-based to retainer<strong>, </strong>despite their limited budget. Below is the production timeline of the project.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/72afb2c670fde5c7afc5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/72afb2c670fde5c7afc5.webp\" width=\"855\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>And to contrast with the earlier harsh comments, we would like to quote a couple of the TD's positive feedback:</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Quote number 1</strong></p></td><td><p>\"Hi Angel*. Concerning Monterey_Cypress, on the visual side, you did really great job, nothing to change, it’s really a very good job.\"</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Quote number 2</strong></p></td><td><p>\"Okay, sorry for the expression but here you kicked my ass, this is just perfect visually , you really got exactly the point for this bush. Amazing job...</p>\n<p>... Overall, very nice work Angel, congratulate your team for me.\"</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><em>*Side</em><em> n</em><em>ote:</em><em> The producer's nick name is Angel.</em><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<h3>Lessons learnt</h3>\n\n<p>This 180 turn from our Xian is truly commendable. They overcame what could've been a severe loss with resilience and expertise. For that reason, we would like to highlight their achievement in this project:1They produced a bunch of SpeedTree experts2They built a flexible project management practice3The team members became more mature and responsible4They created a consistent sharing habit5They managed to reconnect with the client and gained even more trust</p>\n\n<p>Trust is integral to every successful cooperation. Even though the client were harsh at the beginning, they still trusted the team enough to give them a second chance. This level of trust, in turn, brought confidence back to everyone working on the project. If the client kept yelling and being angry, the team's morale would slowly diminish. But, with these measures, we can maintain quality consistency and a good relationship with our clients.<br></p>\n\n<h3>Closing words</h3>\n\n<p>Looking back, the team views the rough start in a positive light. Even though it was a difficult time, it was a worthwhile learning experience that resulted in many unanticipated improvements, including various new processes they are still employing today. We believe their approach to quality control and communication can help every team leverage production and satisfy their clients from the very first day of collaboration.<br></p>\n\n<p>Another valuable lesson we would like to emphasize here is to trust in your team. Believe in them as much as you believe in yourself. Give them a hand, so they have a chance to give theirs back to you. After all, two heads are always better than one.</p>\n\n<p>We hope this story can inspire you to be open to mutual learning, as well as being able to turn negative energy into encouragement.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/0452f928c4c289874280.webp", "assets/library-inline/4ab43f0129569e2c34c5.webp", "assets/library-inline/5372c062593068bf2cea.webp", "assets/library-inline/72afb2c670fde5c7afc5.webp", "assets/library-inline/d648f72f5d6439a09b0f.webp"];
